A tech company is finalizing the design of a new AI assistant. Internal research shows users rate a female-sounding voice as “more pleasant, helpful, and trustworthy” for tasks like scheduling, reminders, and customer support. The team debates whether to set this as the default, with some arguing it reinforces outdated stereotypes of women as subservient helpers, while others say it simply reflects user comfort and practical design.

If you were advising the company’s ethics review panel, what would you recommend?:
"A" = Let users choose at setup, with no suggested default.
"B" = Use a female voice by default—it aligns with natural expectations of supportive, nurturing assistance.
"C" = Avoid a female default—assigning service roles to female voices perpetuates harmful stereotypes.
"D" = Use a gender-neutral voice by default to avoid reinforcing role-based assumptions.
"E" = Offer a female default but include a prompt encouraging users to try other voices.

